        "no_recommendation_release": true,
        "no_external_verification_release": true,
        "two_existing_generation_calls": true,
        "birthday_year_not_invented": true,
        "no_exact_start_invented": true,
        "no_start_preference_invented": true,
        "requested_start_disposition_retained": true
      },
      "reply": "No—these are usually two different ways to get your Medicare coverage, not two policies you buy together. Original Medicare means Parts A and B through the federal program; Medicare Advantage is a private plan that provides your Part A and Part B instead. If you choose Original Medicare, you can add a separate drug plan and possibly Medigap; if you choose Medicare Advantage, you generally do not pair it with Medigap, and many Advantage plans include drug coverage.\n\nWould it help if I gave you a simple side-by-side of how Original Medicare and Medicare Advantage differ in everyday use?",
